  { '@type': 'Question', name: 'Can Kling generate video from an existing image or video?', acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: "Yes. Kling supports image-to-video, and the Kling 3.0 Omni model accepts video/image reference input for element, character, or video-reference generation that keeps content consistent across multi-scene sequences." } },
  { '@type': 'Question', name: 'Does the Kling API work from China?', acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: "Yes - Kling is a Kuaishou first-party product, so mainland developers can access the domestic API without a proxy, which matters for latency-sensitive short-video and e-commerce pipelines. The global klingai.com endpoint serves worldwide creators, though some regions may still require network configuration." } },
  { '@type': 'Question', name: 'How does Kling compare to Luma and Runway?', acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: "Kling is the only one of the three with a China-native path and the only one claiming native 4K video (Luma and Runway top out at 1080p for typical clips). Luma and Runway use credit subscriptions from $12-30/mo; Kling is pure per-second pay-as-you-go with no monthly commitment. Kling 2.5 Turbo previously topped the Artificial Analysis Video Arena ahead of Luma Ray 3." } },
